---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const outcomes = [
  { metric: '60%', label: 'improvement in response and recovery metrics through clearer operating practices' },
  { metric: '3', label: 'combined businesses aligned to shared operating practices' },
  { metric: '1', label: 'India-based team onboarded into the shared operating model' },
];

const operatingModel = [
  {
    number: '01',
    title: 'Make the signal useful',
    body: 'Replace noisy or ambiguous monitoring with dashboards and alerts that help teams understand what is happening and when action is actually required.'
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    title: 'Make the response repeatable',
    body: 'Standardize runbooks, RCA structure, in-incident documentation, escalation paths, and ownership so response quality does not depend on tribal knowledge.'
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    title: 'Automate the coordination',
    body: 'Use workflow automation where it removes avoidable handoffs, including Jira automation that helps incidents and follow-up work move through a consistent ticket lifecycle.'
  },
  {
    number: '04',
    title: 'Build adoption into the model',
    body: 'Train and onboard teams into the same tooling, alerting, runbook, incident, RCA, handoff, and readiness practices so the operating model works beyond the people who created it.'
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Program Delivery & Operating Model | Mike Gilmore"
  description="A case study of improving observability, response ownership, incident practices, automation, governance, and team adoption across technical organizations."
>
  <header class="case-hero shell">
    <div class="case-hero__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case study · Program delivery & operating model</p>
      <h1>Building a shared operating model for reliability work across teams.</h1>
      <p>
        Some reliability problems are technical. Others persist because the signal is noisy, ownership is unclear, documentation is inconsistent, or each team responds differently. I worked on the system around the incidents as deliberately as the incidents themselves.
      </p>
      <p class="case-note">Internal procedures and private operational artifacts are intentionally omitted.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="case-hero__role" aria-label="Role and focus">
      <p class="signal-label">My role</p>
      <p>Program delivery with technical operations context</p>
      <p class="signal-label">Focus</p>
      <p>Observability · ownership · runbooks · RCA · workflow automation · team enablement</p>
    </aside>
  </header>

  <section class="case-outcomes" aria-labelledby="operating-outcomes-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="case-section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">Evidence</p>
        <h2 id="operating-outcomes-heading">The operating model around the work became part of the reliability solution.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric-grid">
        {outcomes.map((outcome) => (
          <div class="case-metric">
            <strong>{outcome.metric}</strong>
            <span>{outcome.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-story" aria-labelledby="operating-context-heading">
    <aside class="case-story__rail">
      <p class="eyebrow">Context</p>
      <p>Combined technical organizations</p>
      <p>Customer-facing operations</p>
      <p>Cross-team adoption and onboarding</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="case-story__body">
      <h2 id="operating-context-heading">Better incident response started with making the right things easier to see and easier to follow.</h2>
      <p>
        I improved dashboards and alerting so the signal was more meaningful, then standardized the response around that signal. Shared runbook templates made procedures easier to use and maintain. A common RCA structure and real-time incident documentation flow made it easier to capture what happened while the context was still fresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also introduced Jira automation to support ticket and follow-up workflow management. The objective was not more process for its own sake; it was to remove ambiguity about what happens next, who owns it, and how the learning survives the incident.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft" aria-labelledby="operating-model-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="case-section-heading case-section-heading--dark">
        <p class="eyebrow">Operating model</p>
        <h2 id="operating-model-heading">Turn good response behavior into a system teams can actually adopt.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="case-flow">
        {operatingModel.map((step) => (
          <li>
            <span class="case-flow__number">{step.number}</span>
            <h3>{step.title}</h3>
            <p>{step.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-story" aria-labelledby="operating-analysis-heading">
    <aside class="case-story__rail">
      <p class="eyebrow">What I contributed</p>
      <p>Program delivery</p>
      <p>Process improvement</p>
      <p>Change management</p>
      <p>Documentation & governance</p>
      <p>Team onboarding</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="case-story__body">
      <h2 id="operating-analysis-heading">The real test of an operating model is whether another team can use it without the original author in the room.</h2>
      <p>
        As teams were brought together, I helped standardize the tooling, alerting, runbooks, incident flow, RCA expectations, handoffs, and readiness practices. That included onboarding an India-based team into the shared approach rather than leaving the standards as documentation that only one group understood.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those changes contributed to a 60% improvement in response and recovery metrics while supporting shared operating practices across three combined businesses.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark" aria-labelledby="operating-takeaway-heading">
    <div class="shell case-takeaway">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">Takeaway</p>
      <h2 id="operating-takeaway-heading">Good operations scale when the signal, ownership, documentation, and learning are all designed to travel across teams.</h2>
      <p>
        The pattern I carry forward is to make the signal clearer, define the next owner, document the response while it is happening, automate coordination that does not need human judgment, and build adoption into the change itself.
      </p>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="button button--secondary case-button-light" href="/work/">Back to selected work</a>
        <a class="button button--secondary case-button-light" href="/resume/">View resume</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
